// Queen's Gambit Declined (the pro repertoire) — the Watch course, one beat a
// move (WO-OPENINGS-REWRITE-01). Written from the fact sheet: masters'
// percentages, the settled engine verdict on each move, the board after it, and
// the facts the app's teaching computers found. The student plays Black.
// Standard: docs/OPENING-WATCH-BEATS.md, said in the think-out-loud register
// (§2a). One story runs through every line: …e6 holds d5 and shuts in the
// bishop on c8, and each line is a different way of getting that bishop out and
// of landing the …c5 break. The last seven tabs are lines taught for this opening
// in the repertoire's videos; where each of the others went is recorded in
// docs/levy-lines/decisions/pro-gothamchess-qgd.json.
import type { WatchCourse } from './watchCourse';

const MAIN = ['d4', 'd5', 'c4', 'e6', 'Nf3', 'Nf6', 'Nc3', 'Be7', 'Bf4', 'O-O', 'e3', 'c5', 'dxc5', 'Bxc5', 'Qc2', 'Nc6', 'a3', 'Qa5', 'Rd1', 'Be7'];

/** The main line's beats, in order — one per move of `MAIN`. */
const MAIN_BEATS: Array<[string, string]> = [
  ["They open with d4, a little over a third of all master games. The pawn takes its share of the center and covers e5, so your e-pawn can't go two squares for free, and it opens the diagonal for their bishop on c1. How do you want to meet it?", 'd4 — center pawn, covers e5.'],
  ["…d5 — about a quarter of the masters; six in ten play …Nf6 first, the move order two of the Catalan tabs on this page use. Your pawn takes its own share of the center. It covers e4, so their e-pawn can't join in for free, and it stops their pawn on d4 where it stands. They are a little better. Now, what do they do about your pawn on d5?", '…d5 — your share of the center.'],
  ["c4 — about three masters in four. This is the Queen's Gambit: the pawn hits d5 from the side, and nothing guards it on c4. So can you just take it? You can, but then e4 comes and they have two pawns side by side in the center. The real question is how you hold d5.", 'c4 — the gambit: hits d5.'],
  ["…e6 — about a third of the masters, and the engine's first choice; half play …c6, the Slav, and one in eight takes on c4. This is the Queen's Gambit Declined. The pawn guards d5, so if they take there, a pawn takes back and you keep your stake in the center. It also opens the diagonal for your bishop on f8. The price? Look at c8: your other bishop is now shut in behind the e6 pawn. Remember that bishop. Getting it out is the thread that runs through this whole course. They are a little better.", '…e6 — guards d5; c8 bishop waits.'],
  ["Nf3 — about four masters in ten; six in ten bring out the other knight first, Nc3, which leads to the Exchange and the tabs after it. The knight develops and covers e5, so your e-pawn stays where it is. Notice what it doesn't do yet: nothing new hits d5.", 'Nf3 — develops, covers e5.'],
  ["…Nf6 — almost two masters in three; about one in five play …c6 here, the first step of the Stonewall tab. Why this square for the knight and not e7? On e7 it would stand in your bishop's way. From f6 it is a third guard on d5, and it covers e4, so their e-pawn can't come forward for free. And keep one idea in your pocket: the bishop on f8 can still go to b4 the moment a knight lands on c3.", '…Nf6 — third guard on d5.'],
  ["Nc3 — about six masters in ten; three in ten play g3 instead, the Catalan, which has its own tabs. Now count the hits on d5: their c4 pawn and this knight, against your queen, the e6 pawn and the knight on f6. Two against three, so the pawn on d5 holds. And there's the knight on c3 you were waiting for: …Bb4 would pin it, and that's the Ragozin tab. This line develops more quietly.", 'Nc3 — second hit on d5.'],
  ["…Be7 — about three masters in ten; …c6 and the bishop sortie to b4 are each played almost as often. Why the modest square? On d6 the bishop could be hit by their c-pawn pushing to c5. On e7 it does one job cleanly: it gets off f8, so you castle next move. Solid first, active later. It's about level.", '…Be7 — off f8, castle next.'],
  ["Bf4 — about four masters in ten, and the engine's first choice; a few more play Bg5, the pin, which the Tartakower tab covers. On f4 the bishop pins nothing. It points down the diagonal at c7, which only your queen guards, and it covers e5 and d6. They are a little better.", 'Bf4 — points at c7, covers e5.'],
  ["You castle, as nearly every master does. Your king is safe before anything opens up, and the rook comes to f8. One loose end: the pawn on c7 is still hit by their bishop and guarded only by your queen. Keep that in mind whenever the queen wants to leave d8.", '…O-O — king safe first.'],
  ["e3 — more than nine masters in ten. It opens the diagonal for the bishop on f1, and look what that does for the pawn on c4: until now nothing guarded it, and now the bishop does. The pawn also guards d4. They are a little better. So the gambit pawn is finally safe, and that makes this the moment for your own break.", 'e3 — the bishop now guards c4.'],
  ["…c5 — about a third of the masters; a few more develop with …Nbd7 first. Here's the break this whole setup was waiting for: the pawn hits d4, right in the center. And see why the quiet bishop move mattered: the bishop on e7 guards c5. If they take, the bishop takes back, lands on an active square in one move, and the c-file is half-open for your rook. If they don't, …cxd4 trades off their center pawn. Either way your position opens up. They are a little better.", '…c5 — the break: hits d4.'],
  ["dxc5 — almost every master. They take, and for a moment they're a pawn up. But the pawn on c5 has no guard, and your bishop on e7 is looking right at it.", 'dxc5 — a pawn up, briefly.'],
  ["…Bxc5 — more than nine masters in ten. Material is level again, and compare this bishop with a move ago: from e7 it has come to an open diagonal, where it hits e3. One thing has changed for them, too. Their d-pawn is gone, so the d-file is half-open on their side, and your d5 pawn is where their pieces will look next. It's about level.", '…Bxc5 — pawn back, bishop active.'],
  ["Qc2 — about a third of the masters; just as many play a3 first. The queen steps off the d-file for a reason: d1 is now free for a rook, and if the d-file ever opens, the queens won't be traded. From c2 it also looks down the diagonal at h7, which your king and the knight on f6 guard.", 'Qc2 — off the d-file, toward h7.'],
  ["…Nc6 — nine masters in ten. In many lines of this opening the knight stays off c6, so the c-pawn can move. Here the c-pawn has already done its work on c5, so the knight takes its best square, covering d4 and e5. And it has an idea of its own. Look at b4: a knight landing there would come with a hit on their queen on c2. Watch what they do about that square.", '…Nc6 — covers d4, looks at b4.'],
  ["a3 — seven masters in ten, and there's their answer. The pawn covers b4, so neither your knight nor your bishop can land there. It also makes b4 a square their own b-pawn could use one day, with a hit on your bishop on c5. Small move, two jobs. It's about level.", 'a3 — takes b4 away.'],
  ["…Qa5 — eight masters in ten. Where does the queen go once your pieces are out? Follow the diagonal from a5 to e1: it runs straight through c3. Their knight is now pinned to their king. A pinned knight can't move, so it doesn't really attack d5 any more. The queen also hits the pawn on a3.", '…Qa5 — pins the knight on c3.'],
  ["Rd1 — about half the masters, and the engine's first choice; a quarter castle long instead. Here's the rook on the square the queen cleared, and it looks straight down the d-file at d5. Count again: the c4 pawn, the knight on c3 and now the rook, against your e6 pawn and the knight on f6. Even with the knight pinned, the pawn on d5 could use one more guard. Where does it come from?", 'Rd1 — third hit on d5.'],
  ["…Be7 — three masters in four, and the engine's first choice. Back to e7? It looks like a wasted move, until you see what the bishop was standing on. On c5 it blocked your queen's view along the fifth rank. Now the queen on a5 guards d5: three guards against three attackers, and one of those attackers is pinned. The bishop also joins the queen against a3. It's about level. Your plan for the middlegame: …Ne4 to trade off the pinned knight on c3, and once the center is settled with …dxc4 or a recapture on d5, your bishop on c8 finally gets a diagonal.", '…Be7 — the queen now guards d5.'],
];

const beats: Record<string, { say: string; sayShort: string }> = {};
MAIN_BEATS.forEach(([say, sayShort], k) => { beats[MAIN.slice(0, k + 1).join(' ')] = { say, sayShort }; });

/** A tab's beats from the first move the main line (or an earlier tab) doesn't share. */
type TabBeats = Array<[string, string]>;

